\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $A(n,d)$ be the adjacency matrix of a uniformly random $d$-regular graph, where $d>2$. Is $A(n,d)$ nonsingular with probability tending to $1$ as $n\to\infty$? What are the limiting distributions of the permanent of a random $\{\pm1\}$-matrix and the determinant of a random symmetric $\{\pm1\}$-matrix?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2014/01}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $A(n,d)$ be the adjacency matrix of a uniformly random $d$-regular graph, where $d>2$. Is $A(n,d)$ nonsingular with probability tending to $1$ as $n\to\infty$? What are the limiting distributions of the permanent of a random $\{\pm1\}$-matrix and the determinant of a random symmetric $\{\pm1\}$-matrix?

